\title{Singleton Induction}
\taxon{Definition}
\import{macros}
\p{
    Let #{A} be a type equipped with a point #{a:A}. We say that #{A}
    satisfies \strong{singleton induction at #{a}} if, for every type family
    #{B:A\to\mathcal{U}}, the evaluation map
    ##{
        \mathsf{ev}_a:
        \left(\Pi_{x:A}B(x)\right)\to B(a),
        \qquad
        \mathsf{ev}_a(s)\defeq s(a),
    }
    has a [section](def-005G). In symbols,
    ##{
        \mathsf{isSingletonInd}(A,a)
        \defeq
        \Pi_{B:A\to\mathcal{U}}\mathsf{sec}(\mathsf{ev}_a^B).
    }
    Equivalently, for every #{B} there are
    ##{
        \mathsf{indSing}_a:B(a)\to\Pi_{x:A}B(x)
    }
    and a homotopy
    ##{
        \mathsf{compSing}_a:
        \mathsf{ev}_a\circ\mathsf{indSing}_a\sim\mathsf{id}_{B(a)}.
    }
    The computation rule is propositional: it identifies
    #{\mathsf{indSing}_a(b,a)} with #{b}, rather than requiring judgmental
    equality.
}
